an7.72:18.2 | S: And while this discourse was being spoken, sixty monks spewed hot blood from their mouths. | T: And while this explanation was being given, approximately sixty monks vomited hot blood,
an7.72:18.3 | S: Sixty mendicants resigned the training and returned to a lesser life, saying: | T: approximately sixty monks renounced the training and returned to the lower life [saying,]
an7.72:18.4 | S: “It’s too hard, Blessed One! It’s just too hard!” | T: “It’s so hard to do, Blessed One, so very hard to do,”
an7.72:18.5 | S: And sixty monks were freed from defilements by not grasping. | T: while the minds of approximately sixty monks, through lack of clinging/sustenance, were released from effluents.
an7.72:18.6 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## snp4.12
snp4.12:1.1 | S: “Each maintaining their own view, | T: “Dwelling on their own views,
snp4.12:1.2 | S: those who are skilled disagree, arguing: | T: quarreling, different skilled people say:
snp4.12:1.3 | S: ‘Whoever sees it this way <j>understands the teaching; | T: ‘Whoever knows this, understands Dhamma.
snp4.12:1.4 | S: those who reject this are inadequate.’ | T: Whoever rejects this, is imperfect.’
snp4.12:2.1 | S: So arguing, they dispute, | T: Thus quarreling, they dispute:
snp4.12:2.2 | S: saying, ‘The other is a fool, an amateur!’ | T: ‘My opponent’s a fool & unskilled.’
snp4.12:2.3 | S: Which of these is a true statement, | T: Which of these statements is true
snp4.12:2.4 | S: for all of them profess to be experts?” | T: when all of them say they are skilled?”
snp4.12:3.1 | S: “If not accepting another’s teaching | T: “If, in not accepting an opponent’s doctrine,
snp4.12:3.2 | S: makes you a useless fool lacking wisdom, | T: one’s a fool, a beast of inferior discernment,
snp4.12:3.3 | S: then they’re all fools lacking wisdom, | T: then all are fools of inferior discernment—
snp4.12:3.4 | S: for they all maintain their own view. | T: all of these who dwell on their views.
snp4.12:4.1 | S: But if having your own view makes you pristine— | T: But if, in siding with a view, one’s cleansed,
snp4.12:4.2 | S: pure in wisdom, expert and thoughtful— | T: with discernment made pure, sensible, skilled,
snp4.12:4.3 | S: then none of them lack wisdom, | T: then none of them are of inferior discernment,
snp4.12:4.4 | S: for such is the view they have all embraced. | T: for all of them have their own views.
snp4.12:5.1 | S: I do not say that it is correct | T: I don’t say, ‘That’s how it is,’
snp4.12:5.2 | S: when they call each other fools. | T: the way fools tell one another.
snp4.12:5.3 | S: Each has built up their own view to be the truth, | T: They each make out their views to be true
snp4.12:5.4 | S: which is why they take the other as a fool.” | T: and so regard their opponents as fools.”
snp4.12:6.1 | S: “What some say is true and correct, | T: “What some say is true —’That’s how it is’—
snp4.12:6.2 | S: others say is hollow and false. | T: others say is ‘falsehood, a lie.’
snp4.12:6.3 | S: So arguing, they dispute; | T: Thus quarreling, they dispute.
snp4.12:6.4 | S: why don’t ascetics say the same thing?” | T: Why can’t contemplatives say one thing & the same?”
snp4.12:7.1 | S: “The truth is one, there is no second, | T: “The truth is one, there is no second
snp4.12:7.2 | S: understanding which folk would not dispute. | T: about which a person who knows it would argue with one who knows.
snp4.12:7.3 | S: But those ascetics each boast of different truths; | T: Contemplatives promote their various own truths,
snp4.12:7.4 | S: that’s why they don’t say the same thing.” | T: that’s why they don’t say one thing & the same.”
snp4.12:8.1 | S: “But why do they speak of different truths, | T: “But why do they say various truths,
snp4.12:8.2 | S: these proponents who profess to be experts? | T: those who say they are skilled?
snp4.12:8.3 | S: Are there really so many different truths, | T: Have they learned many various truths
snp4.12:8.4 | S: or do they just follow their own lines of reasoning?” | T: or do they follow conjecture?”
snp4.12:9.1 | S: “No, there are not many different truths | T: “Apart from their perception there are no many various constant truths in the world.
snp4.12:9.2 | S: that, apart from perception, <j>are lasting in the world. | T: (none)
snp4.12:9.3 | S: Having formed their reasoning <j>regarding different views, | T: Theorizing conjectures with regard to views,
snp4.12:9.4 | S: they say there are two things: true and false. | T: they speak of a pair: true & false.
snp4.12:10.1 | S: The seen, heard, or thought, <j>or precepts or vows— | T: Dependent on what’s seen, heard, & sensed, dependent on habits & practices,
snp4.12:10.2 | S: based on these they show disdain. | T: one shows disdain [for others].
snp4.12:10.3 | S: Standing in judgment, they scoff, | T: Taking a stance on his decisions, praising himself,
snp4.12:10.4 | S: saying, ‘The other is a fool, an amateur!’ | T: he says, ‘My opponent’s a fool & unskilled.’
snp4.12:11.1 | S: They take the other as a fool on the same grounds | T: That by which he regards his opponents as fools
snp4.12:11.2 | S: that they speak of themselves as an expert. | T: is that by which he says he is skilled.
snp4.12:11.3 | S: Professing to be expert on their own authority, | T: Calling himself skilled,
snp4.12:11.4 | S: they disdain the other while saying the same thing. | T: he despises another who speaks the same way.
snp4.12:12.1 | S: They are perfect, <j>according to their own extreme view; | T: Agreeing on a view gone out of bounds,
snp4.12:12.2 | S: drunk on conceit, imagining themselves proficient. | T: drunk with conceit, imagining himself perfect,
snp4.12:12.3 | S: They have anointed themselves in their own mind, | T: he has consecrated, with his own mind, himself
snp4.12:12.4 | S: for such is the view they have embraced. | T: as well as his view.
snp4.12:13.1 | S: If the word of your opponent makes you deficient, | T: If, by an opponent’s word, one’s inferior,
snp4.12:13.2 | S: then they too are lacking wisdom. | T: the opponent’s of inferior discernment as well.
snp4.12:13.3 | S: But if on your own authority <j>you’re a knowledge master, an attentive one, | T: But if, by one’s own word one’s an attainer-of-knowledge, enlightened,
snp4.12:13.4 | S: then there are no fools among the ascetics. | T: no one among contemplatives is a fool.
snp4.12:14.1 | S: ‘Those who proclaim a teaching other than this | T: ‘Those who approve of a doctrine other than this
snp4.12:14.2 | S: have fallen short of purity, and are inadequate’: | T: are lacking in purity, imperfect.’
snp4.12:14.3 | S: so say each one of the sectarians, | T: That’s what the many sectarians say,
snp4.12:14.4 | S: for they are deeply attached to their own view. | T: for they’re smitten with passion for their own views.
snp4.12:15.1 | S: ‘Here alone is purity,’ they say, | T: ‘Only here is there purity,’ that’s what they say.
snp4.12:15.2 | S: denying that there is purification in other teachings. | T: ‘In no other doctrine is purity,’ they say.
snp4.12:15.3 | S: Thus each one of the sectarians, being dogmatic, | T: That’s how the many sectarians are entrenched,
snp4.12:15.4 | S: speaks forcefully within the context <j>of their own journey. | T: speaking firmly there concerning their own path.
snp4.12:16.1 | S: But in that case, so long as they are speaking forcefully <j>of their own journey, | T: Speaking firmly concerning your own path,
snp4.12:16.2 | S: how can they take the other as a fool? | T: what opponent here would you take as a fool?
snp4.12:16.3 | S: They are the ones who provoke conflict | T: You’d simply bring strife on yourself
snp4.12:16.4 | S: when they call the other a fool <j>with an impure teaching. | T: if you said your opponent’s a fool with an impure doctrine.
snp4.12:17.1 | S: Standing in judgment, <j>measuring by their own standard, | T: Taking a stance on your decisions, & yourself as your measure,
snp4.12:17.2 | S: they dispute with the world more and more. | T: you dispute further down into the world.
snp4.12:17.3 | S: But a personage who has given up all judgments | T: But a person who’s abandoned all decisions
snp4.12:17.4 | S: creates no conflict in the world.” | T: creates no strife in the world.”
snp4.12:17.5 | P: Cūḷabyūhasuttaṁ dvādasamaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud1.10
ud1.10:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud1.10:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud1.10:1.3 | S: Now at that time Bāhiya of the Bark Cloth was residing by Suppāraka on the ocean shore, where he was honored, respected, revered, venerated, and esteemed. And he received robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. | T: And on that occasion Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth was living in Suppāraka by the seashore. He was worshipped, revered, honored, venerated, and given homage–a recipient of robes, alms food, lodgings, & medicinal requisites for the sick.
ud1.10:1.4 | S: Then as he was in private retreat this thought came to his mind, | T: Then, when he was alone in seclusion, this line of thinking appeared to his awareness:
ud1.10:1.5 | S: “I am one of those in the world who are perfected or on the path to perfection.” | T: “Now, of those who in this world are arahants or have entered the path of arahantship, am I one?”
ud1.10:2.1 | S: Then a deity who was a former relative of Bāhiya, having sympathy and wanting what’s best for him, approached him and said: | T: Then a devatā who had once been a blood relative of Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth–compassionate, desiring his welfare, knowing with her own awareness the line of thinking that had arisen in his awareness–went to him and on arrival said to him,
ud1.10:2.2 | S: “Bāhiya, you’re not a perfected one, nor on the path to perfection. | T: “You, Bāhiya, are neither an arahant nor have you entered the path of arahantship.
ud1.10:2.3 | S: You don’t have the practice by which you might become a perfected one or one on the path to perfection.” | T: You don’t even have the practice whereby you would become an arahant or enter the path of arahantship.”
ud1.10:3.1 | S: “So who then are those in the world who are perfected or on the path to perfection?” | T: “Then who, in this world with its devas, are arahants or have entered the path to arahantship?”
ud1.10:3.2 | S: “In the northern lands there is a city named Sāvatthī. | T: “Bāhiya, there is a city in the northern country named Sāvatthī.
ud1.10:3.3 | S: There that Blessed One is now staying, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha. | T: There the Blessed One–an arahant, rightly self-awakened–is living now.
ud1.10:3.4 | S: He is a perfected one and teaches the Dhamma for the sake of perfection.” | T: He truly is an arahant and teaches the Dhamma leading to arahantship.”
ud1.10:4.1 | S: Impelled by that deity, Bāhiya left Suppāraka right away. | T: Then Bāhiya, deeply chastened by the devatā, left Suppāraka right then and,
ud1.10:4.2 | S: Sojourning no more than a single night in any place, he made his way to Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery in the Jeta Grove at Sāvatthī. | T: in the space of one night, went all the way to where the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud1.10:4.3 | S: At that time several mendicants were walking mindfully in the open air. | T: Now on that occasion, a large number of monks were doing walking meditation in the open air.
ud1.10:4.4 | S: Bāhiya approached them and said, | T: He went to them and, on arrival, said,
ud1.10:4.5 | S: “Sirs, where is the Blessed One at present, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha? | T: “Where, venerable sirs, is the Blessed One–the arahant, rightly self-awakened–now staying?
ud1.10:4.6 | S: For I want to see him.” | T: We want to see that Blessed One–the arahant, rightly self-awakened.”
ud1.10:4.7 | S: “He has entered an inhabited area for almsfood, Bāhiya.” | T: “The Blessed One has gone into town for alms.”
ud1.10:5.1 | S: Then Bāhiya rushed out of the Jeta Grove and entered Sāvatthī, where he saw the Buddha walking for alms. He was impressive and inspiring, with peaceful faculties and heart, attained to the highest self-control and serenity, like an elephant with tamed, guarded, and controlled faculties. | T: Then Bāhiya, hurriedly leaving Jeta’s Grove and entering Sāvatthī, saw the Blessed One going for alms in Sāvatthī–serene & inspiring serene confidence, calming, his senses at peace, his mind at peace, having attained the utmost tranquility & poise, tamed, guarded, his senses restrained, a Great One (nāga).
ud1.10:5.2 | S: Bāhiya went up to the Buddha, bowed down with his head to the Buddha’s feet, and said, | T: Seeing him, he approached the Blessed One and, on reaching him, threw himself down, with his head at the Blessed One’s feet, and said,
ud1.10:5.3 | S: “Sir, let the Blessed One teach me the Dhamma! Let the Holy One teach me the Dhamma! | T: “Teach me the Dhamma, O Blessed One! Teach me the Dhamma, O One-Well-Gone,
ud1.10:5.4 | S: That would be for my lasting welfare and happiness.” | T: that will be for my long-term welfare & bliss.”
ud1.10:5.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: When this was said, the Blessed One said to him,
ud1.10:5.6 | S: “It’s not the time, Bāhiya, so long as I have entered an inhabited area for almsfood.” | T: “This is not the time, Bāhiya. We have entered the town for alms.”
ud1.10:6.1 | S: For a second time, Bāhiya said, | T: A second time, Bāhiya said to the Blessed One,
ud1.10:6.2 | S: “But you never know, sir, when life is at risk, either the Buddha’s or my own. | T: “But it is hard to know for sure what dangers there may be for the Blessed One’s life, or what dangers there may be for mine.
ud1.10:6.3 | S: Let the Blessed One teach me the Dhamma! Let the Holy One teach me the Dhamma! | T: Teach me the Dhamma, O Blessed One! Teach me the Dhamma, O One-Well-Gone,
ud1.10:6.4 | S: That would be for my lasting welfare and happiness.” | T: that will be for my long-term welfare & bliss.”
ud1.10:6.5 | S: For a second time, the Buddha said, | T: A second time, the Blessed One said to him,
ud1.10:6.6 | S: “It’s not the time, Bāhiya, so long as I have entered an inhabited area for almsfood.” | T: “This is not the time, Bāhiya. We have entered the town for alms.”
ud1.10:7.1 | S: For a third time, Bāhiya said, | T: A third time, Bāhiya said to the Blessed One,
ud1.10:7.2 | S: “But you never know, sir, when life is at risk, either the Buddha’s or my own. | T: “But it is hard to know for sure what dangers there may be for the Blessed One’s life, or what dangers there may be for mine.
ud1.10:7.3 | S: Let the Blessed One teach me the Dhamma! Let the Holy One teach me the Dhamma! | T: Teach me the Dhamma, O Blessed One! Teach me the Dhamma, O One-Well-Gone,
ud1.10:7.4 | S: That would be for my lasting welfare and happiness.” | T: that will be for my long-term welfare & bliss.”
ud1.10:8.1 | S: “In that case, Bāhiya, you should train like this: | T: “Then, Bāhiya, you should train yourself thus:
ud1.10:8.2 | S: ‘In the seen will be merely the seen; in the heard will be merely the heard; in the thought will be merely the thought; in the known will be merely the known.’ | T: In reference to the seen, there will be only the seen. In reference to the heard, only the heard. In reference to the sensed, only the sensed. In reference to the cognized, only the cognized.

